From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Implement Windows.Web.Http (the winget REST/source transport)

winget's source and REST clients talk to the network through the WinRT
Windows.Web.Http stack -- HttpBaseProtocolFilter, HttpClient, HttpRequestMessage,
HttpMethod, HttpStringContent, the typed header collections, and the async
operations that carry the response. Wine implements none of it (windows.web.dll
had only Windows.Data.Json), so winget's `search'/`source' commands aborted the
instant they tried to open a source: RoActivateInstance of
Windows.Web.Http.Filters.HttpBaseProtocolFilter failed and winget dereferenced
the null.

This implements the subset winget exercises, in windows.web.dll:

 - HttpBaseProtocolFilter (http_filter.c) with its cache-control, and, through
   IHttpFilter::SendRequestAsync, the actual transport -- it reads the request's
   method, URI (Wine's Windows.Foundation.Uri), headers and body and drives
   winhttp, then wraps the status and body in an HttpResponseMessage.
 - HttpClient (http_client.c): builds on a filter, merges its default request
   headers into each request, and delegates the send to the filter.
 - HttpMethod (http_method.c), HttpRequestMessage (http_request.c) and
   HttpStringContent / the response body (http_content.c).
 - HttpResponseMessage (http_response.c): status code and content.
 - The typed request-header collections (http_headers.c, http_header_values.c):
   the default interface of each is the typed IHttp*HeaderValueCollection
   (ParseAdd/TryParseAdd), with IVector<T> and IStringable behind it -- so
   winget's `.UserAgent().ParseAdd(...)' and `.Connection().Clear()' both work.
 - A pre-completed async operation (http_async.c): the work is synchronous
   (winhttp blocks), so every IAsyncOperationWithProgress<T,P> is already done;
   put_Completed fires the handler at once, which is what C++/WinRT's blocking
   get() waits for.

With this, real winget-cli sends real HTTPS requests to the package source
(cdn.winget.microsoft.com) and reads the responses. The response-header
collection and the source download follow.

